WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:09.760
So today's focus will be
very much on improving

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:11.360
the models using search.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:14.820
And there can be two kinds
of search that we will see.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:17.000
One in code models, where
we are sampling a lot

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:21.640
and then we are searching based
on that, and then deep research

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:24.280
style search, which you
will see in homework three.

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:26.760
So a lot of what we
will cover in AlphaCode

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:30.800
and AlphaCode 2,
those search patterns

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:32.420
you will use also in homework.

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:35.180
Your homework two is
focused on human eval,

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:39.080
so this will give
you an overview

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:42.640
of what to expect
in simpler problems

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:44.380
and then even more
complex problems.

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:46.760
And then the agent
search, this will

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:49.700
be closer to what we will
give you in homework three.

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:55.600
The broad thing that I want
to say with these models

00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:01.110
is that we know that the
solutions lie in the search

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:04.470
space of the models, but how
do you curate the answer out

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:08.350
of the search space of what
the model outputs is roughly

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:11.710
what we are covering today.

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:15.430
So let's start with AlphaCode.

00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:17.550
So the problem we
want to solve here

00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:22.730
is a more complex version of
the simple coding agent problem.

00:01:22.730 --> 00:01:25.550
So if you use a coding
agent, what you often get

00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:29.570
is some form of an autocomplete,
where you have a line,

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:33.930
and then the coding agent will
complete the line for you.

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:36.870
So this is closer to a
competitive programming problem,

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:40.653
where given a competitive
programming problem,

00:01:40.653 --> 00:01:42.070
you're given a
problem description

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:44.550
and then you're given
some input and outputs,

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:49.310
so you have some a contract in
that programming problem, which

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:51.130
says that if you give
this set of inputs,

00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:53.090
you should get this
set of outputs.

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:58.290
And what you want
to really solve for

00:01:58.290 --> 00:02:01.910
is how do we solve these
competitive programming problems

00:02:01.910 --> 00:02:03.610
so that--

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:05.303
so the things that
we already are

00:02:05.303 --> 00:02:06.970
able to do as code
completions, but when

00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:10.850
we try to do blocks of
code, or solve real problems

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:13.230
end-to-end, that starts to
be a much harder problem,

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:17.730
as you'll see in the ways
that this problem gets solved.

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:23.170
So just to give you a preview
of how AlphaCode came about

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:26.670
and what it achieved, and it
was-- this is an older paper,

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:29.330
but it's worth remembering
that at that point in time,

00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:34.090
when AlphaCode was released,
which is almost four years ago,

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:39.210
it ranked top 54% among
the contest participants

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:41.530
in 10 contests, and
this was the first time

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:43.490
it was shown that
AI can generalize

00:02:43.490 --> 00:02:47.910
beyond just narrow tasks and
solve problems end-to-end.

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:50.690
And this was a
much harder problem

00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:53.770
than what you can do in just
human eval, where you basically

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:57.160
are given a very small problem
like complete this function,

00:02:57.160 --> 00:02:59.418
or you're given instructions
to exactly solve

00:02:59.418 --> 00:03:00.960
what you want to
solve, and you don't

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:03.520
have to reason about,
understanding the problem

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:06.260
and then figuring out what it
would take to solve the problem.

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:08.820
So this was definitely
longer problem descriptions,

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:11.520
longer solution
lengths, and you were

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:13.580
having to understand
from the docstring, what

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:16.080
is the right solution approach
even before going and solving

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:16.580
that?

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:19.680
So it's not just like taking
the problem description

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:22.180
from the programmer and
then just coding it up.

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:31.320
And this is well understood that
when the models are generating

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:34.840
suggestions in one single
line, it's much easier for us

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:38.620
as software developers
to interact with that,

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:40.780
and you get much higher
success rates, for example,

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:44.540
in Copilot and
equivalent functions,

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:47.180
but when you have to
solve problems end-to-end,

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:50.760
that ends up being a
much harder problem.

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:53.860
And I'll go through each of
these block diagrams one by one.

00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:00.542
This is pre having large
models, so they actually

00:04:00.542 --> 00:04:02.000
were pre-training
their own models.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:06.160
The pre-trained models in the
next report will get replaced,

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:08.900
so what you see here is that
you have a stage, where you are

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:10.860
basically coming up with
a model from which you

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:12.060
are going to sample.

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:17.339
Now this model we will later
replace with a large language

00:04:17.339 --> 00:04:17.880
model.

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:21.220
So here, they're also doing
pre-training and fine tuning.

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:24.940
And the data that fed that data
that really helped this model

00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:28.660
train was GitHub data
and then code contests.

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:31.540
What code contest is
basically a large chunk

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:33.620
of problems and
solutions that come

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:35.980
from competitive programming.

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:41.580
So once they trained
the model here,

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:43.440
they're going to sample
a lot of solutions.

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:46.520
So we have seen repeated
sampling before,

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:50.260
so this is repeated sampling,
but at a massive scale,

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:52.580
so you will end up with
a lot of solutions.

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:56.090
And then you're going to play
some tricks to figure out

00:04:56.090 --> 00:04:59.590
which set of solutions
should we even run tests on?

00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:02.650
So, so far, we've just been
closing the loop, where

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:06.130
we take whatever set of
solutions the model outputs

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:08.370
and then running tests on them.

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:10.950
In this particular case, you're
actually going to select,

00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:13.290
so there's a selection
stage before you're

00:05:13.290 --> 00:05:17.410
going to complete and
execute and evaluate.

00:05:17.410 --> 00:05:21.010
So just to walk through
things step-by-step.

00:05:21.010 --> 00:05:24.970
And so the first
step is pre-training.

00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:26.708
You're going to
pre-train a model.

00:05:26.708 --> 00:05:29.250
In this particular case, they're
going to use masked language

00:05:29.250 --> 00:05:30.150
model loss.

00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:33.690
You can also have a decoder only
model, which we'll see shortly,

00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:36.770
and the model is
trained on GitHub code

00:05:36.770 --> 00:05:39.130
about 700 gigabytes
of that trained

00:05:39.130 --> 00:05:42.290
with next token prediction.

00:05:42.290 --> 00:05:44.810
What's interesting is that
when they fine tune it,

00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:48.250
they are using some tricks, so
they're using regularization

00:05:48.250 --> 00:05:50.090
so that they can assign
higher probability

00:05:50.090 --> 00:05:51.430
to more meaningful patterns.

00:05:51.430 --> 00:05:53.810
This is interesting in code.

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:57.750
And then they're doing something
called as value conditioning

00:05:57.750 --> 00:05:58.785
and prediction.

00:05:58.785 --> 00:06:00.910
So the thing that I want
to highlight in this slide

00:06:00.910 --> 00:06:04.170
is that in the
regularization technique,

00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:06.790
when they're doing fine tuning,
they assign higher weight

00:06:06.790 --> 00:06:09.123
to higher likelihood
tokens so that there

00:06:09.123 --> 00:06:11.290
are certain tokens that
will have higher likelihood,

00:06:11.290 --> 00:06:13.750
and then they are putting lower
weight to lower likelihood

00:06:13.750 --> 00:06:15.630
tokens, so this
is called as GOLD,

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:17.290
so it will help
improve the precision.

00:06:17.290 --> 00:06:22.150
So in the next token
prediction loss,

00:06:22.150 --> 00:06:24.470
they are basically adding
a weighting mechanism

00:06:24.470 --> 00:06:28.550
to take into account the
likelihood of the tokens also,

00:06:28.550 --> 00:06:31.950
which helps in
certain ways and helps

00:06:31.950 --> 00:06:35.090
to assign high probabilities
to more meaningful patterns,

00:06:35.090 --> 00:06:38.310
so this is done in
the fine tuning phase.

00:06:38.310 --> 00:06:41.590
So once you get this
model that is able to--

00:06:41.590 --> 00:06:44.257
and this was an encoder, decoder
model that they were initially

00:06:44.257 --> 00:06:46.590
using, and then they also
tried with decoder only models

00:06:46.590 --> 00:06:47.950
in AlphaCode 2.

00:06:47.950 --> 00:06:49.710
Once they get this
model, they are going

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:52.860
to try large scale sampling.

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:55.380
They generate 1 million.

00:06:55.380 --> 00:06:56.880
1 million is a large number.

00:06:56.880 --> 00:07:00.200
They generate 1 million diverse
sample programs per question,

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:02.460
so if you have a question,
a competitive programming

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:05.200
question, they're generating
1 million solutions,

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:09.620
half in Python, half in C++,
so machines can do that.

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:12.660
And they randomize the
problem tags and the ratings

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:15.000
in the prompt, and they use
a high sampling temperature,

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:20.820
so it's like the solutions
are going to be diverse.

00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:23.580
And once they generate these
solutions, what they're doing

00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:26.960
is they are doing some amount
of filtering and clustering.

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:30.620
So they filter the samples to
only those that passed tests

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:32.820
given in the problem
statement, and then they

00:07:32.820 --> 00:07:36.740
are clustering them so that
they're syntactically different,

00:07:36.740 --> 00:07:40.340
but semantically equivalent, so
they differentiate between that.

00:07:40.340 --> 00:07:46.340
So they are basically using
a clustering mechanism,

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:49.760
where they use a separate
test input generation model,

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:52.550
train it to predict test inputs
given problem descriptions,

00:07:52.550 --> 00:07:55.710
and then create these new test
inputs for unseen problems,

00:07:55.710 --> 00:07:57.850
so they're basically
trying to figure out

00:07:57.850 --> 00:08:03.490
different tricks to
cluster this test inputs,

00:08:03.490 --> 00:08:06.898
the semantic equivalence
being one way to do that.

00:08:06.898 --> 00:08:08.690
Now the reason this
clustering is important

00:08:08.690 --> 00:08:12.370
is it is effectively
providing a way for us

00:08:12.370 --> 00:08:15.850
to select which set of
solutions are diverse enough

00:08:15.850 --> 00:08:20.930
for us to then evaluate
to close the loop.

00:08:20.930 --> 00:08:23.690
So now, what you
can do is you can

00:08:23.690 --> 00:08:25.570
submit only a
subset of solutions

00:08:25.570 --> 00:08:28.530
to the Codeforces platform,
which is the actual competition

00:08:28.530 --> 00:08:29.270
platform.

00:08:31.990 --> 00:08:33.909
And that will check the
program correctness.

00:08:33.909 --> 00:08:38.530
It will benchmark against other
best performers on this task

00:08:38.530 --> 00:08:41.570
like human competitors, but
the challenge here is that you

00:08:41.570 --> 00:08:42.950
cannot submit all the solutions.

00:08:42.950 --> 00:08:46.230
So 1 million solutions cannot
be submitted to this platform,

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:49.570
so they have done
a curation phase,

00:08:49.570 --> 00:08:54.350
a filtering and clustering
phase to get to this stage.

00:08:54.350 --> 00:08:56.930
And then there's also
evaluation on code contests.

00:08:56.930 --> 00:08:59.650
Remember, that code contest
was used to train the model,

00:08:59.650 --> 00:09:02.550
so there is a test set
that they hold out,

00:09:02.550 --> 00:09:05.450
which will basically
give them a signal.

00:09:05.450 --> 00:09:07.690
So this is the data set
that is created by authors,

00:09:07.690 --> 00:09:12.190
but they can also measure signal
on this particular data set

00:09:12.190 --> 00:09:17.310
to figure out if their solution
is supposed to do well or not.

00:09:17.310 --> 00:09:20.550
So when they first
did evaluation

00:09:20.550 --> 00:09:26.730
on Codeforces platform, they
took their model, ran it live,

00:09:26.730 --> 00:09:28.870
generated samples,
filtered with example test,

00:09:28.870 --> 00:09:31.070
cluster to get some
submissions, and then

00:09:31.070 --> 00:09:34.310
submitted it to the platform,
and they ran this with--

00:09:34.310 --> 00:09:35.950
they submitted in
10 competitions,

00:09:35.950 --> 00:09:38.750
which each had
5,000 participants.

00:09:38.750 --> 00:09:42.710
And what they found was
that their solutions

00:09:42.710 --> 00:09:47.630
with this AlphaCode actually
had an average ranking of 54.3,

00:09:47.630 --> 00:09:51.180
assuming 10 submissions per
problem, so not 1 million,

00:09:51.180 --> 00:09:56.300
and it was competitive
with 28% of the competitors

00:09:56.300 --> 00:09:58.340
in the last six months.

00:09:58.340 --> 00:10:00.740
So this table is
showing you that if you

00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:03.580
looked at a certain
contest ID, how

00:10:03.580 --> 00:10:05.640
was the AlphaCode model doing?

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:08.020
What was its percentage
ranking, the best ranking

00:10:08.020 --> 00:10:11.100
and the estimated ranking and
the worst ranking relative

00:10:11.100 --> 00:10:13.120
to other programmers?

00:10:13.120 --> 00:10:15.900
This is more of a ranking
based as to once you submit

00:10:15.900 --> 00:10:19.620
a solution, you're ranked
based on the efficiency

00:10:19.620 --> 00:10:21.920
of your solution and the
correctness of your solution.

00:10:26.260 --> 00:10:28.560
Before I move
further, questions?

00:10:34.660 --> 00:10:35.300
Yes.

00:10:35.300 --> 00:10:37.940
You go back to previous page.

00:10:37.940 --> 00:10:39.300
It seems like the--

00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:40.746
This one?

00:10:40.746 --> 00:10:41.620
So numbers.

00:10:41.620 --> 00:10:44.180
In the table, if you
look at the performance

00:10:44.180 --> 00:10:47.160
across different data
sets, it seems that--

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:48.980
This is different contexts.

00:10:48.980 --> 00:10:52.180
It seems they did pretty
well with the final 1623.

00:10:52.180 --> 00:10:56.440
Even the worst ones
like 54% and 1618

00:10:56.440 --> 00:11:01.160
we see the algorithm
is pretty good.

00:11:01.160 --> 00:11:06.105
But if you look at some
others like 1613 and 1615

00:11:06.105 --> 00:11:07.340
one is getting worse.

00:11:07.340 --> 00:11:09.960
It's getting worse by 20%.

00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:17.040
I'm just curious why [INAUDIBLE]
variation of [INAUDIBLE].

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:18.960
I'll ask you that question back.

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:22.340
What do you think?

00:11:22.340 --> 00:11:24.240
This is your pipeline.

00:11:28.880 --> 00:11:31.360
Where will the
variance come from?

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:35.400
So different contexts will
have different problem inputs.

00:11:35.400 --> 00:11:37.280
Maybe it's the two contexts.

00:11:37.280 --> 00:11:39.700
So the two context,
they perform well

00:11:39.700 --> 00:11:44.840
is the context as
similar as possible.

00:11:44.840 --> 00:11:49.230
But there are other
places that [INAUDIBLE]

00:11:49.230 --> 00:11:52.190
somewhere in your context
that the algorithm

00:11:52.190 --> 00:11:56.350
may fail to or struggle to
answer because it [INAUDIBLE].

00:12:01.310 --> 00:12:08.070
The large scale-- so
what's your name, again?

00:12:08.070 --> 00:12:10.230
What he's asking is
why do we get variance

00:12:10.230 --> 00:12:13.830
across different context IDs,
and it does well in one context

00:12:13.830 --> 00:12:15.470
and does not do well in another?

00:12:15.470 --> 00:12:16.990
So at the end of
the day, you are

00:12:16.990 --> 00:12:23.990
depending on how close are
these say, contest problems?

00:12:23.990 --> 00:12:25.090
In distribution, they are.

00:12:25.090 --> 00:12:27.430
So that's one
plausible hypothesis.

00:12:27.430 --> 00:12:31.870
The other plausible hypothesis
is that our ability to--

00:12:31.870 --> 00:12:33.510
so in the large
scale sampling, that

00:12:33.510 --> 00:12:36.790
would control like is one
of the solutions correct?

00:12:36.790 --> 00:12:40.070
But then our ability to select
