So that's the
percentage of problems.

00:14:18.460 --> 00:14:21.280
So if you get the model
to generate k samples

00:14:21.280 --> 00:14:24.960
and you submit all of them for
evaluation on the hidden test,

00:14:24.960 --> 00:14:29.880
how many problems will actually
solve the problem description

00:14:29.880 --> 00:14:31.520
that was given?

00:14:31.520 --> 00:14:34.840
It measures how good is the
search aspect of the sampling

00:14:34.840 --> 00:14:37.240
process, so it's basically
coverage in the lectures

00:14:37.240 --> 00:14:39.680
that we covered before.

00:14:39.680 --> 00:14:45.950
What this particular
benchmark measures is 10@k.

00:14:45.950 --> 00:14:47.830
10 being the number
of submissions

00:14:47.830 --> 00:14:50.350
that you're going to go
submit for evaluation.

00:14:50.350 --> 00:14:54.210
So even if you get the
model to output k samples,

00:14:54.210 --> 00:14:57.550
in this particular
case, 1 million,

00:14:57.550 --> 00:15:00.510
you don't have the ability
to measure correctness

00:15:00.510 --> 00:15:03.730
on all the samples for the
final set of solutions,

00:15:03.730 --> 00:15:07.470
so you do have to do some
selection, or scoring

00:15:07.470 --> 00:15:09.890
to decide which set of
solutions I'm going to submit.

00:15:09.890 --> 00:15:12.950
So it measures also
the filtering process

00:15:12.950 --> 00:15:15.255
of how well the
model will behave

00:15:15.255 --> 00:15:17.130
when you have a very
large number of samples,

00:15:17.130 --> 00:15:19.130
so you're not just
generating samples,

00:15:19.130 --> 00:15:21.150
but you have to do
some search yourself

00:15:21.150 --> 00:15:24.470
before you can submit the
solutions for evaluation

00:15:24.470 --> 00:15:26.670
to hidden tasks.

00:15:26.670 --> 00:15:28.410
Now, why does this matter?

00:15:28.410 --> 00:15:32.050
So this is validation
set and that's test set.

00:15:32.050 --> 00:15:36.350
And they train different model
sizes, so this is 9B model,

00:15:36.350 --> 00:15:39.090
41B model, so that's
a larger model,

00:15:39.090 --> 00:15:41.690
and then 41B with clustering.

00:15:41.690 --> 00:15:45.370
And what they're presenting
is validation set results

00:15:45.370 --> 00:15:51.290
for 10@1k, so this is the
question you were asking me.

00:15:51.290 --> 00:15:54.330
They're basically going to
submit 10 solutions out of 1k

00:15:54.330 --> 00:15:59.250
generated outputs, and then keep
increasing that to 10k, 100k,

00:15:59.250 --> 00:16:00.450
and 1 million.

00:16:00.450 --> 00:16:02.170
And then they're going
to do similar set

00:16:02.170 --> 00:16:03.390
for test set as well.

00:16:06.370 --> 00:16:09.970
And what you will notice is
that typically, the larger model

00:16:09.970 --> 00:16:13.170
size consistently will do better
than the smaller model size.

00:16:13.170 --> 00:16:16.130
That is something we
have talked about before.

00:16:16.130 --> 00:16:18.210
But the other thing to
notice is that as you

00:16:18.210 --> 00:16:20.490
go for larger number
of samples, which

00:16:20.490 --> 00:16:24.030
is something we have seen as
well, you are able to do better.

00:16:24.030 --> 00:16:27.910
So going from 10@1k
to 10@1 million,

00:16:27.910 --> 00:16:31.050
you're clearly doing better,
and same on the test set side.

00:16:31.050 --> 00:16:33.530
And the clustering.

00:16:33.530 --> 00:16:39.440
So typically, we had covered
this slide with pass@ number

00:16:39.440 --> 00:16:40.520
of samples.

00:16:40.520 --> 00:16:45.760
In this particular case, even
clustering is consistently--

00:16:45.760 --> 00:16:50.340
so 10@1k will definitely also
improve similar to pass@1k.

00:16:50.340 --> 00:16:52.420
And then if you look
at the last row here,

00:16:52.420 --> 00:16:56.040
which is 41B plus clustering,
you see that that's consistently

00:16:56.040 --> 00:16:56.580
better.

00:16:56.580 --> 00:17:00.600
So like 9B, versus 41B,
versus 41B plus clustering.

00:17:00.600 --> 00:17:03.360
The 41B plus clustering is
definitely going to still be

00:17:03.360 --> 00:17:07.119
better than the one before
because it definitely provides

00:17:07.119 --> 00:17:09.160
an improvement.

00:17:09.160 --> 00:17:11.720
And the reason for doing
that is because if you

00:17:11.720 --> 00:17:14.013
were to measure the
set of solutions

00:17:14.013 --> 00:17:15.680
that the model has
output and you wanted

00:17:15.680 --> 00:17:18.560
to measure their diversity, the
clustering provides you a way

00:17:18.560 --> 00:17:21.300
to submit only the
diverse set of solutions,

00:17:21.300 --> 00:17:24.040
if you can only
submit 10 problems.

00:17:24.040 --> 00:17:26.679
And since competitive coding
problems are quite difficult,

00:17:26.679 --> 00:17:30.200
you don't get much
benefit out of submitting

00:17:30.200 --> 00:17:33.380
the same set of solutions
to the evaluation process,

00:17:33.380 --> 00:17:37.320
so if you can choose which
set of problems to submit,

00:17:37.320 --> 00:17:38.200
that helps.

00:17:42.700 --> 00:17:50.100
And if we were to measure just
10@k and pass@k, if you had--

00:17:50.100 --> 00:17:53.140
so pass@k would be unlimited
attempts and 10@k would be 10

00:17:53.140 --> 00:17:55.900
attempts on evaluating
per problem.

00:17:55.900 --> 00:18:00.320
On the x-axis, you have
the sampling budget,

00:18:00.320 --> 00:18:03.300
and on the y-axis,
you have this accuracy

00:18:03.300 --> 00:18:06.520
in terms of how many attempts
you get to solve the problem.

00:18:06.520 --> 00:18:10.320
The solve rate definitely scales
linearly with more samples,

00:18:10.320 --> 00:18:12.980
so this is very much in line
with what we had seen for pass@k

00:18:12.980 --> 00:18:14.200
in repeated sampling.

00:18:14.200 --> 00:18:15.900
So even after the
selection stage,

00:18:15.900 --> 00:18:20.700
we definitely see the same log
linear trend with more samples.

00:18:20.700 --> 00:18:23.920
And better models have better
slopes, so this particular case,

00:18:23.920 --> 00:18:28.620
you see the 41B, which is the
blue curve, has better solve--

00:18:28.620 --> 00:18:32.220
Sorry, the purple curve, 41B,
has better slope compared

00:18:32.220 --> 00:18:35.820
to the 300 million parameter
model at the bottom,

00:18:35.820 --> 00:18:38.890
and same applies
even for pass@k.

00:18:38.890 --> 00:18:42.810
And then the final thing is
that when only 10 samples are

00:18:42.810 --> 00:18:48.230
submitted, then you do get
bottlenecked in certain ways,

00:18:48.230 --> 00:18:51.470
so you can get higher accuracy.

00:18:51.470 --> 00:18:55.590
So if you compare the
y-axis 10@k versus pass@k,

00:18:55.590 --> 00:18:58.090
if you have unlimited
attempts per problem,

00:18:58.090 --> 00:19:02.250
which is a synthetic scenario,
you are almost getting

00:19:02.250 --> 00:19:04.930
to something above
40%, and here,

00:19:04.930 --> 00:19:07.330
you are only getting to 30%,
so there is definitely some

00:19:07.330 --> 00:19:09.630
bottlenecking happening
in selection stage.

00:19:09.630 --> 00:19:12.650
So you are bottlenecked by
how you filter and cluster

00:19:12.650 --> 00:19:17.290
in some ways, when you have to
choose which set of solutions

00:19:17.290 --> 00:19:18.430
will pass the test.

00:19:21.690 --> 00:19:23.270
Yes.

00:19:23.270 --> 00:19:26.320
I'm just wondering if
this trend continue,

00:19:26.320 --> 00:19:31.690
you really got this tool
another six or more.

00:19:31.690 --> 00:19:32.970
That would be 1 trillion.

00:19:32.970 --> 00:19:35.310
If you really get 1
trillion as the score,

00:19:35.310 --> 00:19:37.990
this attracting
demo will be double.

00:19:37.990 --> 00:19:43.550
So I think that we really need
to just make it 1 trillion.

00:19:43.550 --> 00:19:47.270
We can just double
the performance.

00:19:47.270 --> 00:19:49.230
Well, we're going
to AlphaCode 2.

00:19:49.230 --> 00:19:51.910
Making the model better is
a slightly easier access

00:19:51.910 --> 00:19:55.010
than scaling the number of
samples that you're going after.

00:19:55.010 --> 00:19:59.210
So on AlphaCode 2, the
model performance improves,

00:19:59.210 --> 00:20:01.590
and that helps you reduce
the number of samples

00:20:01.590 --> 00:20:03.450
to get the same performance
or at 1 million,

00:20:03.450 --> 00:20:05.290
you'll get a much
better performance.

00:20:05.290 --> 00:20:08.530
So this is an older model,
but it's a weaker model.

00:20:08.530 --> 00:20:12.470
But the trends generally hold.

00:20:12.470 --> 00:20:16.100
And in some ways, there a--

00:20:16.100 --> 00:20:19.650
I mean, the larger models have
done better generally speaking,

00:20:19.650 --> 00:20:21.810
but there's a model
capability gap.

00:20:21.810 --> 00:20:24.590
So if the base models
are not strong enough,

00:20:24.590 --> 00:20:27.230
they're going to hit
limits at a certain point.

00:20:27.230 --> 00:20:30.150
So the problem is if you really
have no problem with the sample

00:20:30.150 --> 00:20:30.870
budget.

00:20:30.870 --> 00:20:34.545
So you can really be confident
in the whole purpose,

00:20:34.545 --> 00:20:39.300
or you can just really do
one bit of a three example

00:20:39.300 --> 00:20:40.460
with this graph.

00:20:40.460 --> 00:20:43.660
If the log linear trend
continues then yes.

00:20:43.660 --> 00:20:47.580
That might be a project idea
worth exploring if the costs are

00:20:47.580 --> 00:20:48.640
not prohibitive.

00:20:54.580 --> 00:20:55.120
Yes.

00:20:55.120 --> 00:20:56.260
I just want to say
I think that could

00:20:56.260 --> 00:20:58.780
be really useful if you think
that one problem is really

00:20:58.780 --> 00:20:59.340
important.

00:20:59.340 --> 00:21:01.120
You can just forward your--

00:21:01.120 --> 00:21:03.740
All you can do is
solving that problem.

00:21:03.740 --> 00:21:06.740
According too, yeah.

00:21:06.740 --> 00:21:08.460
I think the challenge
for that, which

00:21:08.460 --> 00:21:12.560
is something that is worth
also-- that's a good comment.

00:21:12.560 --> 00:21:15.740
So one of the challenges
with solving it that way

00:21:15.740 --> 00:21:18.720
is that you kind of assume
that if we sample more,

00:21:18.720 --> 00:21:22.060
the diversity
continues to increase.

00:21:22.060 --> 00:21:24.720
So one of the challenges that
we will see in AlphaGo 2,

00:21:24.720 --> 00:21:26.740
and I'll emphasize
again, is that you

00:21:26.740 --> 00:21:29.260
do have to ensure that as
you sample more and more,

00:21:29.260 --> 00:21:31.440
you are getting more
diverse solutions.

00:21:31.440 --> 00:21:33.080
And part of the
process of clustering

00:21:33.080 --> 00:21:35.480
is that it's
effectively figuring out

00:21:35.480 --> 00:21:37.180
which are the diverse
set of solutions.

00:21:37.180 --> 00:21:40.877
So if you sample 10x more
than what was sampled here,

00:21:40.877 --> 00:21:42.960
that you don't end up with
more diverse solutions,

00:21:42.960 --> 00:21:46.880
then you're actually
not going to improve.

00:21:46.880 --> 00:21:50.260
Is there a reason why we have
a log linear relationship here?

00:21:50.260 --> 00:21:55.160
Why is it not any other
modeling of y versus x?

00:21:55.160 --> 00:21:58.960
So this was covered earlier,
I think the passer k

00:21:58.960 --> 00:22:00.420
versus unlimited attempts.

00:22:00.420 --> 00:22:03.200
This is theoretically derivable.

00:22:03.200 --> 00:22:05.800
And I think the main
thing that I'm emphasizing

00:22:05.800 --> 00:22:07.500
is that even after
the selection phase,

00:22:07.500 --> 00:22:10.680
that log linear trend holds.

00:22:10.680 --> 00:22:14.048
The passer k versus sample
budget, this was covered in--

00:22:14.048 --> 00:22:15.840
I don't know the lecture
number, but Azalea

00:22:15.840 --> 00:22:18.640
covered this in that paper
actually theoretically derives

00:22:18.640 --> 00:22:19.140
it.

00:22:19.140 --> 00:22:21.840
This is the large
language monkey's paper

00:22:21.840 --> 00:22:24.480
or the one right after that.

00:22:24.480 --> 00:22:27.640
Yeah, but if you look
at the derivation,

00:22:27.640 --> 00:22:29.460
we can work through
that offline.

00:22:36.148 --> 00:22:39.590
OK, So if we look
at takeaways, I

00:22:39.590 --> 00:22:42.630
think the fun aspect
here is that we

00:22:42.630 --> 00:22:45.937
can get high
coverage by sampling,

00:22:45.937 --> 00:22:48.270
and then-- so this large-scale
sampling, this filtering,

00:22:48.270 --> 00:22:52.310
and the clustering approach,
you can get higher coverage.

00:22:52.310 --> 00:22:56.830
And they were able to
actually also make sure

00:22:56.830 --> 00:22:59.370
that the model is not just
copying existing solutions.

00:22:59.370 --> 00:23:00.290
It actually reasons.

00:23:00.290 --> 00:23:02.207
So they did some search
over the training data

00:23:02.207 --> 00:23:05.790
and actually saw that there
was-- the generated code

00:23:05.790 --> 00:23:06.810
had some novelty.

00:23:06.810 --> 00:23:09.170
So this was actually
generalization out

00:23:09.170 --> 00:23:10.445
of distribution.

00:23:10.445 --> 00:23:12.070
The challenge with
these particular set

00:23:12.070 --> 00:23:14.510
of approaches that they noticed
that's worth highlighting

00:23:14.510 --> 00:23:17.370
is that they were training
the model on loss.

00:23:17.370 --> 00:23:20.090
And loss is often a poor
proxy for solve rates,

00:23:20.090 --> 00:23:23.040
so because there are
many solutions that

00:23:23.040 --> 00:23:24.290
could have solved the problem.

00:23:24.290 --> 00:23:26.830
So they did see that
this model did not

00:23:26.830 --> 00:23:29.860
do well on say, dynamic
programming or constructive

00:23:29.860 --> 00:23:31.560
algorithms in some ways.

00:23:31.560 --> 00:23:35.120
So it was not the strong
in certain domains.

00:23:35.120 --> 00:23:38.380
And then the other aspect
was that it was requiring

00:23:38.380 --> 00:23:40.200
a large-scale set of sampling.

00:23:40.200 --> 00:23:43.460
So that was not ideal in
terms of how practical this

00:23:43.460 --> 00:23:45.900
is to be used everywhere.

00:23:48.980 --> 00:23:54.020
If there was a time bound,
then you can imagine.

00:23:54.020 --> 00:23:57.960
So if you basically were
just examining accuracy,

00:23:57.960 --> 00:23:59.780
then you want to make
unlimited attempts.

00:23:59.780 --> 00:24:03.580
But if you want to do
time-limited bound in terms

00:24:03.580 --> 00:24:06.620
of efficiency, then you do want
to rank acceptable solutions

00:24:06.620 --> 00:24:08.080
as they get generated.

00:24:08.080 --> 00:24:10.260
So that becomes
really important.

00:24:10.260 --> 00:24:15.380
And as I already mentioned,
the more difficult domains

00:24:15.380 --> 00:24:16.640
required more experiments.

00:24:16.640 --> 00:24:20.180
So this was not like-- this
solution approach actually

00:24:20.180 --> 00:24:22.460
wasn't working well
on difficult problems.

00:24:22.460 --> 00:24:26.620
And in fact, there, it's
both a matter of you

00:24:26.620 --> 00:24:28.640
might need more steps
to solve the problem.

00:24:28.640 --> 00:24:30.680
So this particular
approach in one shot

00:24:30.680 --> 00:24:32.820
did not do a
reasonably strong job.

00:24:32.820 --> 00:24:38.320
So multi-step solution
approaches would do better here.

00:24:38.320 --> 00:24:41.440
So this was AlphaCode summary.

00:24:41.440 --> 00:24:42.400
Now, let's see.

00:24:42.400 --> 00:24:43.420
What can we do better?

00:24:43.420 --> 00:24:45.000
So this was like 2022.

00:24:45.000 --> 00:24:48.720
If you look at the next set
of paper which is AlphaCode 2,

00:24:48.720 --> 00:24:50.780
they improved upon
this substantially.

00:24:50.780 --> 00:24:53.240
And that was really helpful.

00:24:53.240 --> 00:24:57.160
And we can also learn
what were the differences.

00:24:57.160 --> 00:24:59.600
And that really helps us
understand what pushes

00:24:59.600 --> 00:25:01.320
the frontier even further.

00:25:01.320 --> 00:25:04.480
So AlphaCode 2 started
with a hypothesis of what

00:25:04.480 --> 00:25:06.700
if you don't
pre-train your model?

00:25:06.700 --> 00:25:08.200
You use an existing LLM.

00:25:08.200 --> 00:25:10.840
So in this particular case,
this work was at Google.

00:25:10.840 --> 00:25:16.880
So they decided to use
Gemini Pro instead of using

00:25:16.880 --> 00:25:18.700
in training their own model.

00:25:18.700 --> 00:25:22.520
And then, they want to customize
it to get a better performance

00:25:22.520 --> 00:25:27.190
on this particular problem set
on competitive programming.

00:25:27.190 --> 00:25:29.070
So the first set of
changes that were made

00:25:29.070 --> 00:25:31.370
were that instead
of pre-training,

00:25:31.370 --> 00:25:33.970
you basically are just going
to fine tune Gemini Pro.

00:25:33.970 --> 00:25:35.250
So it's not just prompting.

00:25:35.250 --> 00:25:36.950
They are fine tuning the model.

00:25:36.950 --> 00:25:40.310
And then, in terms of
sampling and evaluation

00:25:40.310 --> 00:25:43.470
for the large-scale sampling,
they want to ensure diversity.

00:25:43.470 --> 00:25:47.950
So for diversity, they actually
had multiple AlphaGo 2 models

00:25:47.950 --> 00:25:49.770
that allow for massive sampling.

00:25:49.770 --> 00:25:52.470
So when they did and
go and do fine tuning,

00:25:52.470 --> 00:25:55.870
they actually had multiple
variants of this model

00:25:55.870 --> 00:25:58.615
so that they get diversity
in the output sampling.

00:25:58.615 --> 00:25:59.990
And then the third
thing they did

00:25:59.990 --> 00:26:03.330
was that to select the
subset of candidates,

00:26:03.330 --> 00:26:04.410
they had a scoring model.

00:26:04.410 --> 00:26:08.610
So you can see that
as a reward model,

00:26:08.610 --> 00:26:11.210
which was used for obtaining
the best candidates.

00:26:11.210 --> 00:26:13.910
So it's not just based on
clustering and filtering,

00:26:13.910 --> 00:26:16.390
which is a heuristic
for saying OK, this

00:26:16.390 --> 00:26:21.050
would be semantically
equivalent,

00:26:21.050 --> 00:26:24.550
but there's diversity in syntax.

00:26:24.550 --> 00:26:26.970
They are actually now going
for a scoring function

00:26:26.970 --> 00:26:29.390
which can learn that function.

00:26:29.390 --> 00:26:31.250
So it's a learned
approximation of what

00:26:31.250 --> 00:26:36.690
should be given high score and
what should be given low score.

00:26:36.690 --> 00:26:40.630
So the new ideas here,
as I highlighted,

00:26:40.630 --> 00:26:44.130
was A, that they are going
to fine-tune a Gemini Pro

00:26:44.130 --> 00:26:46.550
model to score correctness
before submitting.

00:26:46.550 --> 00:26:48.570
So that's a reward model.

00:26:48.570 --> 00:26:50.730
And they also had
a family of models

00:26:50.730 --> 00:26:52.790
based on different
hyperparameters,

00:26:52.790 --> 00:26:55.810
so different difficulty
levels, different tags.

00:26:55.810 --> 00:26:58.590
So they fine-tune several models
with varied hyperparameters,

00:26:58.590 --> 00:27:00.890
and then that maximizes
the diversity of the number

00:27:00.890 --> 00:27:02.850
of samples they generate.

00:27:02.850 --> 00:27:04.710
And they also improve
their data sets.

00:27:04.710 --> 00:27:07.370
So the data set that
they fine-tune on

00:27:07.370 --> 00:27:10.697
is V2 version of CodeContests,
which is actually open source.

00:27:10.697 --> 00:27:12.530
And then, they had
another high-quality data

00:27:12.530 --> 00:27:14.110
set for scoring purposes.

00:27:16.730 --> 00:27:21.950
So going back to this
diagram, what changes next?

00:27:21.950 --> 00:27:24.800
So let's take a
more detailed look.

00:27:24.800 --> 00:27:29.800
So here, if you look at the
input model on the fine-tuning

00:27:29.800 --> 00:27:31.480
stage, you have
this new data set

00:27:31.480 --> 00:27:38.260
called CodeContests V2, which
is going to be used to generate.

00:27:38.260 --> 00:27:39.900
Now, you're going
to do some tagging.

00:27:39.900 --> 00:27:43.000
So there's some amount just
like, take your CodeContests V2

00:27:43.000 --> 00:27:44.640
data set and put--

00:27:44.640 --> 00:27:48.120
bisect it or rather segment
it into different data sets

00:27:48.120 --> 00:27:51.800
