00:41:21.960 --> 00:41:22.520
That's--

00:41:30.520 --> 00:41:33.640
What if you can identify
a simpler problem,

00:41:33.640 --> 00:41:37.800
maybe you can run
with less samples.

00:41:37.800 --> 00:41:38.900
Yes, exactly.

00:41:38.900 --> 00:41:42.017
So if the model--

00:41:42.017 --> 00:41:43.600
so this is something
that was probably

00:41:43.600 --> 00:41:45.975
seen in the repetitive sampling
and the test-time compute

00:41:45.975 --> 00:41:48.000
as well, is to-- if you
have simpler problems,

00:41:48.000 --> 00:41:50.040
then it's likely that
you can get coverage

00:41:50.040 --> 00:41:51.460
with fewer number of samples.

00:41:51.460 --> 00:41:53.720
So you don't have to
go to 1 million samples

00:41:53.720 --> 00:41:54.660
every single time.

00:41:54.660 --> 00:41:58.080
It's almost like
saying the model does--

00:41:58.080 --> 00:42:00.240
more likely to generate
the solution in the search

00:42:00.240 --> 00:42:03.600
space of what it outputs.

00:42:03.600 --> 00:42:10.300
So it should be easier to sample
or its initial set of solutions

00:42:10.300 --> 00:42:14.500
with some iteration, with some
iterative refinement should

00:42:14.500 --> 00:42:17.260
be easier to correct, as
opposed to having to generate

00:42:17.260 --> 00:42:18.720
a lot of different solutions.

00:42:18.720 --> 00:42:21.100
One of the gains that
you get if you generate

00:42:21.100 --> 00:42:24.780
a lot of parallel
solutions is that you're

00:42:24.780 --> 00:42:27.380
trying to get the model
to provide diversity

00:42:27.380 --> 00:42:29.580
in the approaches,
and you're not

00:42:29.580 --> 00:42:32.240
trying to get it to fix
what it already output.

00:42:35.260 --> 00:42:37.220
What about the question four?

00:42:37.220 --> 00:42:39.600
How can we embed reasoning
directly into the model?

00:42:43.900 --> 00:42:45.547
OK, let's try out.

00:42:45.547 --> 00:42:46.880
Let's see what you already said.

00:42:46.880 --> 00:42:49.020
Maybe you can have more
on the training side.

00:42:49.020 --> 00:42:50.437
By dealing with
the training side,

00:42:50.437 --> 00:42:53.300
you can take all
the comments off.

00:42:53.300 --> 00:42:57.140
You've done enough problems
so you know in the second one

00:42:57.140 --> 00:43:00.620
how it already feels after, like
the framework of the solution.

00:43:00.620 --> 00:43:04.130
Instead of one, you know it's
very probably to the problems

00:43:04.130 --> 00:43:05.830
that we mentioned
in that framework.

00:43:05.830 --> 00:43:07.530
And that is slightly--

00:43:07.530 --> 00:43:10.490
it's slightly different than the
standard of directly for when

00:43:10.490 --> 00:43:13.690
solving being the change
or we'll be solving.

00:43:13.690 --> 00:43:16.850
We have the final solution and
the bit of the final solution.

00:43:16.850 --> 00:43:19.890
So if we don't have
enough common data,

00:43:19.890 --> 00:43:23.970
then we use the LLM to
test if there's a code

00:43:23.970 --> 00:43:26.195
to see the first
section is about what,

00:43:26.195 --> 00:43:27.570
the second section
is about what,

00:43:27.570 --> 00:43:28.970
the third section about what.

00:43:28.970 --> 00:43:32.410
So it's-- the training data
is that part of the training.

00:43:32.410 --> 00:43:35.211
The data is only the
framework, the set

00:43:35.211 --> 00:43:36.878
where it's that few
steps with that form

00:43:36.878 --> 00:43:41.290
under that pre-training and
that space between the same.

00:43:41.290 --> 00:43:42.530
OK.

00:43:42.530 --> 00:43:44.210
So what Geoffrey is
saying is that you

00:43:44.210 --> 00:43:47.930
add hints in your training data
set around the solution of what

00:43:47.930 --> 00:43:50.850
kind of algorithm is being
used to solve the problem.

00:43:50.850 --> 00:43:53.250
What's a more generic
version of that?

00:43:53.250 --> 00:43:55.950
What have we already covered
in that line of work?

00:43:59.090 --> 00:44:03.840
As programmers, you think
before you write the answer.

00:44:03.840 --> 00:44:05.700
What if that thinking
could be captured?

00:44:08.902 --> 00:44:10.360
If you added those
chain of thought

00:44:10.360 --> 00:44:13.560
as part of your training
set or if you remember

00:44:13.560 --> 00:44:16.760
the STAR approach where
you're getting the model

00:44:16.760 --> 00:44:20.120
to generate the solution and
then give the hint as the answer

00:44:20.120 --> 00:44:23.160
and then ask it to come up
with that reasoning, then

00:44:23.160 --> 00:44:26.960
that can also be part of how
the model embeds it, right?

00:44:31.720 --> 00:44:32.860
Any other ideas?

00:44:35.920 --> 00:44:38.220
I think the other interesting
thing that was mentioned,

00:44:38.220 --> 00:44:39.820
which is probably
worth highlighting,

00:44:39.820 --> 00:44:42.760
is that if you have a more
complex problem than you might

00:44:42.760 --> 00:44:46.280
want to decompose the
problem into subparts

00:44:46.280 --> 00:44:50.520
and add hints for subparts
as opposed to trying

00:44:50.520 --> 00:44:53.660
to solve the problem in one go,
because these are hard problems.

00:44:57.480 --> 00:44:58.020
Yes.

00:44:58.020 --> 00:45:00.440
It's about what you tackled
that for the standalone

00:45:00.440 --> 00:45:01.940
but that's sequential.

00:45:01.940 --> 00:45:06.337
Maybe you give-- you succeed on
the first intermediate output,

00:45:06.337 --> 00:45:08.420
and you have something
forward that is never going

00:45:08.420 --> 00:45:10.660
to make the second part work.

00:45:10.660 --> 00:45:15.900
So how does that sequential or
how can you decompose that work?

00:45:15.900 --> 00:45:20.020
So I think all of these are
hinting at we do need to close

00:45:20.020 --> 00:45:23.620
the loop at some point in a
multi-step fashion and almost

00:45:23.620 --> 00:45:26.740
want a tree search style of
solution where you basically

00:45:26.740 --> 00:45:29.160
want the--

00:45:29.160 --> 00:45:31.780
I basically sample for,
say, the first step,

00:45:31.780 --> 00:45:32.960
and is that roughly correct?

00:45:32.960 --> 00:45:34.600
And you want to sample
for the second step,

00:45:34.600 --> 00:45:36.460
and then you want to
be able to backtrack.

00:45:36.460 --> 00:45:39.220
So it's roughly heading
in that direction,

00:45:39.220 --> 00:45:44.280
but it's early work
that gives you hints.

00:45:44.280 --> 00:45:46.000
So it's basically
got on your thinking,

00:45:46.000 --> 00:45:48.660
and then perhaps that's
your project, right?

00:45:48.660 --> 00:45:50.460
But your question
is very relevant

00:45:50.460 --> 00:45:54.260
as to what happens
if your answer is

00:45:54.260 --> 00:45:56.500
correct in the first step,
but then the next step

00:45:56.500 --> 00:45:59.540
is not correct?

00:45:59.540 --> 00:46:02.690
And the task
decomposition is often

00:46:02.690 --> 00:46:06.550
relevant in that most problems
have a certain way of solving.

00:46:06.550 --> 00:46:09.310
So if there is common instructed
patterns, then that's doable.

00:46:09.310 --> 00:46:11.090
But if you do
expect patterns that

00:46:11.090 --> 00:46:14.170
are out of distribution,
then maybe,

00:46:14.170 --> 00:46:18.090
you do need human in the loop.

00:46:18.090 --> 00:46:20.730
That's like a
scientist style of work

00:46:20.730 --> 00:46:24.230
that folks covered last lecture.

00:46:31.210 --> 00:46:32.770
OK?

00:46:32.770 --> 00:46:33.290
OK?

00:46:33.290 --> 00:46:35.170
Let's come to the last part.

00:46:35.170 --> 00:46:39.090
So we spent a lot
of time on just code

00:46:39.090 --> 00:46:45.202
and how to select a
set of code samples

00:46:45.202 --> 00:46:49.830
that can pass and solve
competitive coding problems.

00:46:49.830 --> 00:46:52.970
Now, let's look at how
would you do something

00:46:52.970 --> 00:46:55.130
like building a
deep research agent?

00:46:55.130 --> 00:46:58.950
So Search-o1 is basically going
to use large reasoning models

00:46:58.950 --> 00:47:02.150
as the base and then try
to build a deep research

00:47:02.150 --> 00:47:03.690
agents on top of that.

00:47:03.690 --> 00:47:06.190
And it's worth paying
attention because you

00:47:06.190 --> 00:47:08.750
will use this in homework 3.

00:47:08.750 --> 00:47:12.150
So the key idea here is that
you want to use large reasoning

00:47:12.150 --> 00:47:15.150
models to retrieve
based on a query

00:47:15.150 --> 00:47:16.530
that you want to give the model.

00:47:16.530 --> 00:47:20.290
So large reasoning models
have impressive reasoning,

00:47:20.290 --> 00:47:22.390
but as you know that
most of these models

00:47:22.390 --> 00:47:24.230
are trained on
with some knowledge

00:47:24.230 --> 00:47:26.350
cut off dates, so they
typically will not

00:47:26.350 --> 00:47:28.290
have the freshest knowledge.

00:47:28.290 --> 00:47:30.550
If something happened
yesterday, the model

00:47:30.550 --> 00:47:32.370
will need to go
look up that data.

00:47:32.370 --> 00:47:36.150
It's not going to be
there in the model.

00:47:36.150 --> 00:47:39.270
The other challenge when you
work with reasoning models, per

00:47:39.270 --> 00:47:43.190
se, is that they will go
and output a lot of tokens

00:47:43.190 --> 00:47:46.070
in long-form reasoning, but
when they have knowledge gaps,

00:47:46.070 --> 00:47:51.330
they will be using terms that
are expressing uncertainty.

00:47:51.330 --> 00:47:56.990
So one example is that if you go
use benchmark on GPQA data set,

00:47:56.990 --> 00:48:01.320
you will see the term perhaps
in the reasoning chains a lot,

00:48:01.320 --> 00:48:06.380
or you will see the term
alternatively or wait or--

00:48:06.380 --> 00:48:09.060
so they're basically
expressing that the knowledge

00:48:09.060 --> 00:48:12.620
gaps will show up as uncertainty
in the way the model is

00:48:12.620 --> 00:48:13.400
thinking.

00:48:13.400 --> 00:48:16.283
So you want to bootstrap
that in some way,

00:48:16.283 --> 00:48:17.700
because otherwise,
those knowledge

00:48:17.700 --> 00:48:20.200
gaps will continue to propagate
through the entire reasoning

00:48:20.200 --> 00:48:21.500
chain.

00:48:21.500 --> 00:48:24.820
Now, one simple solution based
on learning from feedback

00:48:24.820 --> 00:48:26.820
or learning from tool
calls is that why don't we

00:48:26.820 --> 00:48:32.740
just take this query and
get a relevant document?

00:48:32.740 --> 00:48:35.140
So you take this query,
pass it to a search tool,

00:48:35.140 --> 00:48:38.020
and get the relevant
document, and then

00:48:38.020 --> 00:48:41.860
put that document in the prompt,
and then get the model to output

00:48:41.860 --> 00:48:43.020
based on that.

00:48:43.020 --> 00:48:44.200
Why is that not enough?

00:48:44.200 --> 00:48:47.580
So that would be more like
retrieval augmented generation.

00:48:47.580 --> 00:48:51.580
So whatever is your question,
you generate a query,

00:48:51.580 --> 00:48:54.760
and you get a document, and
you put it in the prompt,

00:48:54.760 --> 00:48:58.720
and then that's what you
use to retrieve the answer.

00:48:58.720 --> 00:49:01.800
Now, the challenge with
that sort of simple approach

00:49:01.800 --> 00:49:04.520
is that you retrieve
once at the beginning.

00:49:04.520 --> 00:49:06.940
So you don't have the
ability to tweak things.

00:49:06.940 --> 00:49:10.200
And it's possible that
if your search problem is

00:49:10.200 --> 00:49:13.200
complex enough, then
each reasoning step

00:49:13.200 --> 00:49:15.760
will need different
pieces of information.

00:49:15.760 --> 00:49:18.940
So if you have a problem, which
is asking just the weather,

00:49:18.940 --> 00:49:20.960
then maybe a single
search call is enough,

00:49:20.960 --> 00:49:23.640
but if you're asking to solve
a complex problem, which

00:49:23.640 --> 00:49:26.325
has multiple parts
to solving it,

00:49:26.325 --> 00:49:28.700
then after it gets through a
certain amount of reasoning,

00:49:28.700 --> 00:49:31.020
it needs to go look up
additional information.

00:49:31.020 --> 00:49:34.200
So for complex reasoning, it's
extremely hard to get it right,

00:49:34.200 --> 00:49:36.440
and same for
multi-step reasoning.

00:49:36.440 --> 00:49:38.640
So typically,
retrieval augmented

00:49:38.640 --> 00:49:41.720
generation will improve--

00:49:41.720 --> 00:49:43.700
will show improvement
over direct reasoning,

00:49:43.700 --> 00:49:46.800
but in multi-step reasoning,
it definitely suffers.

00:49:46.800 --> 00:49:49.600
So the solution that
Search-o1 proposes

00:49:49.600 --> 00:49:55.430
is that the model generates
the queries on the go.

00:49:55.430 --> 00:49:57.890
So whenever it sees
these knowledge gaps,

00:49:57.890 --> 00:49:59.170
it will trigger a query.

00:49:59.170 --> 00:50:02.470
It will generate a
search query of what

00:50:02.470 --> 00:50:07.030
tool calls to generate
and go search things over.

00:50:07.030 --> 00:50:09.230
And there will be
multiple iterations

00:50:09.230 --> 00:50:12.710
in a single reasoning session of
like fetching these documents.

00:50:12.710 --> 00:50:14.810
And the second thing
it will do differently,

00:50:14.810 --> 00:50:16.850
and I will show
examples of that,

00:50:16.850 --> 00:50:19.210
is that it will analyze
the retrieved documents.

00:50:19.210 --> 00:50:22.847
So instead of just dumping what
it retrieved as the document,

00:50:22.847 --> 00:50:24.430
it will analyze the
retrieved document

00:50:24.430 --> 00:50:28.430
and extract relevant chunks
of information from there.

00:50:28.430 --> 00:50:32.830
And it will only put
these relevant information

00:50:32.830 --> 00:50:36.590
into the prompts so
that it integrates well

00:50:36.590 --> 00:50:40.230
into the reasoning chain.

00:50:40.230 --> 00:50:44.230
So just to reiterate
how this works--

00:50:44.230 --> 00:50:45.870
so this is an original
question, which

00:50:45.870 --> 00:50:48.910
is a chemistry question,
which is talking about--

00:50:48.910 --> 00:50:52.470
it wants to get the carbon
atoms count of product 3

00:50:52.470 --> 00:50:56.540
which is output of a bunch of
different chemical reactions.

00:50:56.540 --> 00:50:59.740
So if you were to use a
large reasoning model,

00:50:59.740 --> 00:51:01.700
you would ask the model
to start thinking.

00:51:01.700 --> 00:51:04.500
It looks at this term,
which I don't understand,

00:51:04.500 --> 00:51:06.280
and perhaps the model
doesn't understand.

00:51:06.280 --> 00:51:10.340
So it will go ask a
lookup, what does it mean?

00:51:10.340 --> 00:51:12.760
Now, one trick is that
it doesn't go look up,

00:51:12.760 --> 00:51:15.340
so it makes a guess and
comes up with whatever

00:51:15.340 --> 00:51:17.740
is in its knowledge base
in the stored weights

00:51:17.740 --> 00:51:21.120
and provides a final answer
based on what it guessed.

00:51:21.120 --> 00:51:24.760
But if there is a guess here,
and it got the answer wrong,

00:51:24.760 --> 00:51:26.700
then those things
will cascade as errors

00:51:26.700 --> 00:51:28.660
into whatever is
the final answer.

00:51:28.660 --> 00:51:31.100
So all the perhaps or
all the uncertainty

00:51:31.100 --> 00:51:33.120
will basically cascade
into the final answer.

00:51:33.120 --> 00:51:37.100
So your certainty for
the final answer is low.

00:51:37.100 --> 00:51:39.060
Now, one possibility
is that you basically--

00:51:39.060 --> 00:51:41.320
whenever you encounter
unfamiliar knowledge,

00:51:41.320 --> 00:51:42.760
you can now do web searches.

00:51:42.760 --> 00:51:45.300
So that would be
a simple scenario

00:51:45.300 --> 00:51:48.900
of like OK, unfamiliar
term, basically go and do

00:51:48.900 --> 00:51:53.880
a tool call for search, return
the document that basically

00:51:53.880 --> 00:51:56.620
has that search tool for perhaps
there is a Wikipedia page,

00:51:56.620 --> 00:51:59.080
or there's a web page
that has that information,

00:51:59.080 --> 00:52:03.040
and put that in the reasoning
chain, and now do the--

00:52:03.040 --> 00:52:06.120
put that in the prompt, and
then do the call again, and then

00:52:06.120 --> 00:52:08.880
provide the final
answer based on that.

00:52:08.880 --> 00:52:12.160
The only challenge with this
is that as you basically

00:52:12.160 --> 00:52:14.940
increase your amount of
content you're putting there,

00:52:14.940 --> 00:52:17.440
there might be not as
much relevant content

00:52:17.440 --> 00:52:19.020
in these documents.

00:52:19.020 --> 00:52:21.600
Like if you went and fetched
like 10 documents that

00:52:21.600 --> 00:52:24.400
were related to the
information, there's

00:52:24.400 --> 00:52:26.500
too much information for
the model to process,

00:52:26.500 --> 00:52:28.860
so it might actually not
still give the correct answer.

00:52:28.860 --> 00:52:32.120
So in this particular case,
it was like 10 carbon atoms

00:52:32.120 --> 00:52:33.100
in product 3.

00:52:33.100 --> 00:52:34.460
Here's 14 carbon atoms.

00:52:34.460 --> 00:52:38.480
So it still got it wrong.

00:52:38.480 --> 00:52:41.920
What Search-o1 is proposing
is that now you have--

00:52:41.920 --> 00:52:44.080
whenever you get the
unfamiliar knowledge,

00:52:44.080 --> 00:52:47.420
it will go and search
for helpful information.

00:52:47.420 --> 00:52:50.320
So that's still a tool
call to your search.

00:52:50.320 --> 00:52:53.730
But then, it will reason
within the document.

00:52:53.730 --> 00:52:56.070
So it will try to look
at these documents

00:52:56.070 --> 00:52:58.490
and see if this is helpful
information or not,

00:52:58.490 --> 00:53:01.910
extract the relevant
content, and then put it back

00:53:01.910 --> 00:53:05.230
in the next prompt, like
what is the relevant chunk

00:53:05.230 --> 00:53:06.870
of information to put?

00:53:06.870 --> 00:53:10.110
And that will allow the model
to continue coherent reasoning

00:53:10.110 --> 00:53:11.690
and provide the
final answer here.

00:53:11.690 --> 00:53:13.670
So in this particular
case, basically,

00:53:13.670 --> 00:53:17.470
the ability to do this
summarization of information

00:53:17.470 --> 00:53:20.470
or reason within the
document really improves

00:53:20.470 --> 00:53:23.310
the quality of life, how it
gets to the final answer instead

00:53:23.310 --> 00:53:29.350
of clustering the prompt with
10 documents or 20 documents.

00:53:29.350 --> 00:53:31.350
Questions on what's
the difference

00:53:31.350 --> 00:53:32.730
between the three approaches?

00:53:36.950 --> 00:53:37.830
Yes.

00:53:37.830 --> 00:53:40.310
Why in the final leg there
does it say-- in the first one,

00:53:40.310 --> 00:53:41.710
it says--

00:53:41.710 --> 00:53:44.670
or perhaps, it may look at
as far as this little one.

00:53:44.670 --> 00:53:46.270
Does it concern you?

00:53:46.270 --> 00:53:48.130
Because it's been
given the information.

00:53:48.130 --> 00:53:50.410
So the uncertainty has reduced.

00:53:50.410 --> 00:53:53.610
It has actually been-- it's not
even saying that in the middle

00:53:53.610 --> 00:53:54.490
one, which--

00:53:54.490 --> 00:53:59.410
because it's been given the
right information in the prompt.

00:53:59.410 --> 00:54:05.250
I think the harder part is, how
does it know what to search for?

00:54:05.250 --> 00:54:07.630
It needs to know which
keywords it does not know.

00:54:12.450 --> 00:54:15.574
That's the question I would ask.

00:54:15.574 --> 00:54:17.890
It gets to know his answer.

00:54:17.890 --> 00:54:19.490
The answer is I
think you basically

00:54:19.490 --> 00:54:23.410
need to break down what are the
key-- so there is different ways

00:54:23.410 --> 00:54:25.890
to tackle that question, right?

00:54:25.890 --> 00:54:28.370
If you're building
a real application,

00:54:28.370 --> 00:54:32.130
you want to identify
which parts--

00:54:32.130 --> 00:54:34.628
you don't want to rely on the
knowledge base of the LLM.

00:54:34.628 --> 00:54:36.170
And you actually do
want to do a tool

00:54:36.170 --> 00:54:39.210
call because that
information is likely not

00:54:39.210 --> 00:54:43.450
what you want to trust the
weights of the LLM for.

00:54:43.450 --> 00:54:46.370
It's almost like you're
finding the key entities

00:54:46.370 --> 00:54:51.340
in your question and fetching
information on those.

00:54:59.520 --> 00:55:00.360
OK.

00:55:00.360 --> 00:55:02.660
So there were two things
that we are covering here.

00:55:02.660 --> 00:55:05.000
So one is the Agentic
RAG, and one is Search-o1.

00:55:05.000 --> 00:55:07.680
So I'll cover both here.

00:55:07.680 --> 00:55:10.040
Let's first understand
Agentic RAG because then,

00:55:10.040 --> 00:55:13.280
we build on top of that
in the Search-o1 process.

00:55:13.280 --> 00:55:16.120
In Agentic RAG, what you're
doing is you're basically--

00:55:16.120 --> 00:55:21.380
every time you are hitting
entities that you care about,

00:55:21.380 --> 00:55:24.040
you're basically going to
insert special tokens where you

00:55:24.040 --> 00:55:25.720
are going to trigger a search.

00:55:25.720 --> 00:55:27.220
And then when you
get that document,

00:55:27.220 --> 00:55:29.300
you're going to
insert those results.

00:55:29.300 --> 00:55:32.600
So model is generating
some output tokens.

00:55:32.600 --> 00:55:35.600
When it's seeing certain amount
of uncertainty in its output

00:55:35.600 --> 00:55:37.520
tokens, it's generating
these search queries

00:55:37.520 --> 00:55:39.180
between the special tokens.

00:55:39.180 --> 00:55:41.740
And based on that, it goes
and does the tool call.

00:55:41.740 --> 00:55:44.355
And the retrieval
tool will essentially

00:55:44.355 --> 00:55:46.480
return those documents,
which will then be inserted

00:55:46.480 --> 00:55:47.840
into the reasoning chain.

00:55:47.840 --> 00:55:50.180
So this is happening in
large reasoning models

00:55:50.180 --> 00:55:52.460
as part of the whole
reasoning process of like,

00:55:52.460 --> 00:55:56.380
it's also calling tools as
part of the reasoning process.

00:55:56.380 --> 00:56:01.180
So that's one way to solve it.

00:56:01.180 --> 00:56:03.620
But the documents
can be lengthy,

00:56:03.620 --> 00:56:05.000
and they can contain noise.

00:56:05.000 --> 00:56:07.140
And oftentimes,
depending on how good

00:56:07.140 --> 00:56:10.140
the long-context understanding
and reasoning of the model is,

00:56:10.140 --> 00:56:13.200
they might-- actually, if
you put 10 or 20 documents,

00:56:13.200 --> 00:56:16.100
it might actually not be able
to do a good job at reasoning

00:56:16.100 --> 00:56:16.980
over them.

00:56:16.980 --> 00:56:20.220
So the reason in documents
module of Search-o1

00:56:20.220 --> 00:56:23.580
will allow you to do analysis
of each of the documents of how

00:56:23.580 --> 00:56:26.020
relevant the content
is, and then it

00:56:26.020 --> 00:56:29.780
considers the current query,
what it was reasoning previously

00:56:29.780 --> 00:56:34.980
plus this chunk of information
that it extracted, and appends

00:56:34.980 --> 00:56:37.820
that to the prompt to
allow the model to continue

00:56:37.820 --> 00:56:39.740
reasoning based on that.

00:56:39.740 --> 00:56:42.220
So it's essentially
a multi-step process

00:56:42.220 --> 00:56:45.295
within that retrieval
tool function itself

00:56:45.295 --> 00:56:47.670
because it's basically getting
that document information,

00:56:47.670 --> 00:56:49.490
but then also doing
an extraction process

00:56:49.490 --> 00:56:50.810
on top of that.

00:56:50.810 --> 00:56:52.730
It's as almost as humans.

00:56:52.730 --> 00:56:54.690
You don't want to
just go and curate

00:56:54.690 --> 00:56:56.030
all the possible references.

00:56:56.030 --> 00:56:58.250
You also want to take
notes on those references

00:56:58.250 --> 00:57:02.530
so that you have some way of
doing a better job understanding

00:57:02.530 --> 00:57:04.650
and synthesizing
that information.

00:57:04.650 --> 00:57:08.330
And this allows the model to
do a better job at reasoning

00:57:08.330 --> 00:57:10.930
when we are adding
external knowledge.

00:57:10.930 --> 00:57:12.670
And especially for
complex queries,

00:57:12.670 --> 00:57:14.610
this becomes quite important.

00:57:14.610 --> 00:57:15.230
Yes.

00:57:15.230 --> 00:57:18.110
How is it pausing
previous reasoning?

00:57:18.110 --> 00:57:20.290
And if there's any
corrections needed

00:57:20.290 --> 00:57:23.930
for the previous reasoning,
how does it handle corrections?

00:57:23.930 --> 00:57:26.730
So there is an assumption
that there is a state

00:57:26.730 --> 00:57:30.170
or there is a memory
buffer that's keeping that.

00:57:30.170 --> 00:57:33.470
It's not obvious in the
way this is structured,

00:57:33.470 --> 00:57:37.450
but there is basically
some form of notion of this

00:57:37.450 --> 00:57:39.190
is the search query
I'm trying to answer

00:57:39.190 --> 00:57:43.190
and some buffer of where is the
previous reasoning and so on.

00:57:43.190 --> 00:57:44.390
All of it in the slide.

00:57:44.390 --> 00:57:46.560
Yeah.

00:57:46.560 --> 00:57:47.060
Yes.

00:57:47.060 --> 00:57:53.470
[INAUDIBLE] to prepare some new
form in the Agentic RAG module.

00:57:53.470 --> 00:57:57.190
On top of that, we just ask
the model to do some summation

00:57:57.190 --> 00:57:59.950
and then it continues reasoning.

00:57:59.950 --> 00:58:09.750
So is that directly transferred
to the second style like--

00:58:09.750 --> 00:58:14.490
I mean, does this
directly mix method

00:58:14.490 --> 00:58:17.870
to be the new method
proposed when it's due?

00:58:17.870 --> 00:58:21.470
Say that part again, or you
can just speak up louder.

00:58:21.470 --> 00:58:26.270
I mean, if you just modify
the prompt in the Agentic RAG

00:58:26.270 --> 00:58:29.310
module, like you ask the
model to do some summation

00:58:29.310 --> 00:58:33.550
and then continue the
reasoning, does that

00:58:33.550 --> 00:58:37.870
produce the same result
on recent models?

00:58:37.870 --> 00:58:39.730
So I think that's
a great question.

00:58:39.730 --> 00:58:42.430
And I would encourage you
folks to actually try that out

00:58:42.430 --> 00:58:43.620
in homework 3.

00:58:43.620 --> 00:58:45.700
I think the assumption
there is that if you

00:58:45.700 --> 00:58:50.860
were to issue that prompt, you
are assuming that the context

00:58:50.860 --> 00:58:53.260
length of the model and the
reasoning over that context

00:58:53.260 --> 00:58:55.540
length is working really well.

00:58:55.540 --> 00:58:59.360
This is basically Agentic
Context Engineering-style paper.

00:58:59.360 --> 00:59:02.260
So it's basically-- there's a
recent paper called "Agentic

00:59:02.260 --> 00:59:03.280
Context Engineering."

00:59:03.280 --> 00:59:05.540
So essentially,
the limitation here

00:59:05.540 --> 00:59:07.980
is coming from what can
you put in context length

00:59:07.980 --> 00:59:10.120
that the model is going to
do a really good job at.

00:59:13.060 --> 00:59:15.980
In ideal world, we
would want that.

00:59:15.980 --> 00:59:17.920
That's where we want to shoot.

00:59:17.920 --> 00:59:22.540
And hopefully, by the end of the
year, that's where we will be.

00:59:22.540 --> 00:59:23.980
OK.

00:59:23.980 --> 00:59:25.520
So let's look at the example.

00:59:25.520 --> 00:59:27.965
So we were covering this
example of carbon atom

00:59:27.965 --> 00:59:30.340
count in product 3 based on
a bunch of different chemical

00:59:30.340 --> 00:59:31.500
reactions.

00:59:31.500 --> 00:59:33.100
In vanilla reasoning,
the challenge

00:59:33.100 --> 00:59:35.400
was that it was
guessing the structure.

00:59:35.400 --> 00:59:37.100
So it definitely
got wrong answer.

00:59:37.100 --> 00:59:40.880
In Agentic RAG, because you were
just retrieving the documents,

00:59:40.880 --> 00:59:44.810
they ended up being too lengthy,
so it did disrupt the reasoning.

00:59:44.810 --> 00:59:47.730
In Search-o1 process,
it did search

00:59:47.730 --> 00:59:51.130
for this particular
formula, and then

00:59:51.130 --> 00:59:52.870
it refined it to a
certain structure,

00:59:52.870 --> 00:59:55.430
and then that integrated
cleanly into reasoning.

00:59:55.430 --> 00:59:59.570
So essentially, the notion of
extracting the right information

00:59:59.570 --> 01:00:02.610
from the documents
really helps Search-o1

01:00:02.610 --> 01:00:07.010
and get to the correct answer
in this particular case.

01:00:07.010 --> 01:00:09.090
Now, if you were to
go beyond the example

01:00:09.090 --> 01:00:12.070
to results, what we're plotting
is different domains, physics,

01:00:12.070 --> 01:00:14.730
chemistry, biology, and overall.

01:00:14.730 --> 01:00:20.310
And you're plotting
pass at 1 on the y-axis,

01:00:20.310 --> 01:00:23.130
and then you're plotting
the number of documents

01:00:23.130 --> 01:00:25.310
that you get on x-axis.

01:00:25.310 --> 01:00:29.610
So typically, as you increase
the number of documents,

01:00:29.610 --> 01:00:32.090
for direct reasoning
and for RAG,

01:00:32.090 --> 01:00:33.672
the accuracy does not go up.

01:00:33.672 --> 01:00:35.130
But in this particular
case, as you

01:00:35.130 --> 01:00:36.505
increase the number
of documents,

01:00:36.505 --> 01:00:40.628
you are actually able to do
better because you can summarize

01:00:40.628 --> 01:00:43.170
what is relevant information or
not, in this particular case,

01:00:43.170 --> 01:00:45.630
assuming that the documents--
fetching more documents

01:00:45.630 --> 01:00:47.790
gives you more relevant
information related

01:00:47.790 --> 01:00:51.310
to the problem that
you're solving.

01:00:51.310 --> 01:00:54.187
So this is basically
another axes of--

01:00:54.187 --> 01:00:55.770
instead of doing
iterative refinement,

01:00:55.770 --> 01:00:58.103
you can do parallel refinement
because you can basically

01:00:58.103 --> 01:01:00.870
go fetch more information
and then chunk it down

01:01:00.870 --> 01:01:02.730
to put it in the model context.

01:01:05.430 --> 01:01:13.590
Now, if you were to compare the
performance on GPQA data set--

01:01:13.590 --> 01:01:17.890
so if you just compared GPQA
data set in physics, chemistry,

01:01:17.890 --> 01:01:18.570
and biology--

01:01:18.570 --> 01:01:21.390
I was showing you a
plot in the last slide--

01:01:21.390 --> 01:01:24.590
human experts in physics,
chemistry, and biology on GPQA

01:01:24.590 --> 01:01:25.950
would score this much.

01:01:25.950 --> 01:01:28.910
If you look at the reasoning
models and you build Search-o1

01:01:28.910 --> 01:01:33.310
and on top of them, what
you notice is that--

01:01:33.310 --> 01:01:37.830
for example, in
physics and chemistry,

01:01:37.830 --> 01:01:40.540
Search-o1 actually is quite
competitive, sometimes

01:01:40.540 --> 01:01:42.560
even better than what
human experts would do.

01:01:42.560 --> 01:01:45.780
These are hard
problems, by the way.

01:01:45.780 --> 01:01:48.860
And in biology, it's
almost competitive

01:01:48.860 --> 01:01:52.340
to say the biology is
solving the problem.

01:01:52.340 --> 01:01:54.060
One comment I want
to make is that you

01:01:54.060 --> 01:01:57.400
want to focus on physicists
solving physics problems.

01:01:57.400 --> 01:01:59.380
So you want to focus
on the diagonal here.

01:01:59.380 --> 01:02:03.380
It's maybe not obvious like
if you change the domain then.

01:02:03.380 --> 01:02:05.660
That's the number
you're comparing to.

01:02:05.660 --> 01:02:08.460
So the Search-o1 is
doing pretty well

01:02:08.460 --> 01:02:13.460
in physics here and in biology
here, and in chemistry,

01:02:13.460 --> 01:02:15.860
not so much.

01:02:15.860 --> 01:02:21.900
[INAUDIBLE] this Search-o1
perform even better

01:02:21.900 --> 01:02:24.060
than most physicists do this.

01:02:24.060 --> 01:02:26.040
So what's the level
of this physicists?

01:02:26.040 --> 01:02:28.700
[CHUCKLES]

01:02:28.700 --> 01:02:33.820
Maybe they didn't do a good
job sourcing the physicists.

01:02:33.820 --> 01:02:35.780
I think we're definitely
getting to a point

01:02:35.780 --> 01:02:39.120
where the models are able to
do well in certain domains

01:02:39.120 --> 01:02:40.320
more than the--

01:02:40.320 --> 01:02:42.760
but this is not
to say that you're

01:02:42.760 --> 01:02:44.060
outperforming human experts.

01:02:44.060 --> 01:02:45.960
It's more to say that
you're competitive

01:02:45.960 --> 01:02:51.680
with the human experts for
