01:05:26.690 --> 01:05:28.756
That's a great question.

01:05:28.756 --> 01:05:31.770
Why did the-- precision
is the-- or the retrieval

01:05:31.770 --> 01:05:35.000
itself is above
it, not the model.

01:05:35.000 --> 01:05:36.680
So I think there is
an assumption here

01:05:36.680 --> 01:05:40.380
that you did draw at least
for the way I am explaining.

01:05:40.380 --> 01:05:42.760
I mean, it's possible that
you didn't retrieve the right

01:05:42.760 --> 01:05:45.140
documents, but if you look
at the set of questions,

01:05:45.140 --> 01:05:48.040
I think the main claim that this
paper is making is that if you

01:05:48.040 --> 01:05:51.360
find multiple relevant documents
that are loosely correlated

01:05:51.360 --> 01:05:53.820
to what you're querying
about, even then,

01:05:53.820 --> 01:05:56.200
once you put all of those
documents in the context line,

01:05:56.200 --> 01:05:57.240
then that's--

01:05:57.240 --> 01:06:00.440
you're asking the model to do
a lot in terms of reasoning

01:06:00.440 --> 01:06:01.700
over that many documents.

01:06:01.700 --> 01:06:04.220
So that's the claim that this
particular paper is making.

01:06:09.280 --> 01:06:13.440
So in terms of if you were to
look at the takeaways here,

01:06:13.440 --> 01:06:16.280
the gains are definitely
coming from the fact

01:06:16.280 --> 01:06:18.920
that it's able to
reduce the uncertainty.

01:06:18.920 --> 01:06:25.340
And the document quality or what
you basically refine knowledge,

01:06:25.340 --> 01:06:26.700
both of those improve.

01:06:26.700 --> 01:06:31.480
And in the multi-turn scenario,
once you can figure out

01:06:31.480 --> 01:06:36.110
what you don't know it's
able to-- even after refining

01:06:36.110 --> 01:06:38.750
across documents, it can
do this iterative loop

01:06:38.750 --> 01:06:40.090
of improving upon that.

01:06:40.090 --> 01:06:41.670
So it can construct
better searches

01:06:41.670 --> 01:06:43.730
over multi-turn scenarios.

01:06:43.730 --> 01:06:47.990
So in this particular
case, the reasoning quality

01:06:47.990 --> 01:06:49.710
improves substantially
because you're

01:06:49.710 --> 01:06:51.575
providing more
relevant documents,

01:06:51.575 --> 01:06:52.950
and then you're
also figuring out

01:06:52.950 --> 01:06:55.950
where the uncertainty
is over multiple steps.

01:06:55.950 --> 01:06:59.515
So effectively, you have
the right documents.

01:06:59.515 --> 01:07:00.890
And then you can
correct yourself

01:07:00.890 --> 01:07:03.470
because when you put the
documents in there, if you're

01:07:03.470 --> 01:07:06.110
still seeing uncertainty, then
you have additional searches

01:07:06.110 --> 01:07:07.270
to go for.

01:07:07.270 --> 01:07:09.630
So there were some
numbers that the paper

01:07:09.630 --> 01:07:13.890
gives of the number of
perhaps or wait or likely.

01:07:13.890 --> 01:07:15.870
Basically, anything that
quantifies uncertainty

01:07:15.870 --> 01:07:18.630
in the reasoning chains
goes down substantially

01:07:18.630 --> 01:07:19.865
in this particular approach.

01:07:19.865 --> 01:07:21.990
So they're basically
analyzing the reasoning chains

01:07:21.990 --> 01:07:24.750
of the model.

01:07:24.750 --> 01:07:27.870
So just to summarize
what we covered

01:07:27.870 --> 01:07:33.010
here is that when we are
building doing deep research

01:07:33.010 --> 01:07:37.090
agents on top of, say, the
large reasoning models, that's

01:07:37.090 --> 01:07:39.770
a very effective way
to bridge the knowledge

01:07:39.770 --> 01:07:41.790
gaps that exist in
large language models.

01:07:41.790 --> 01:07:44.410
But just doing simple
retrieval augmented

01:07:44.410 --> 01:07:46.150
generation is often not enough.

01:07:46.150 --> 01:07:50.210
And even with just fetching
the documents, using tool calls

01:07:50.210 --> 01:07:51.550
is not enough.

01:07:51.550 --> 01:07:54.430
You need to be able to do that
effectively over multiple turns,

01:07:54.430 --> 01:07:57.890
and then identify the
relevant information to refine

01:07:57.890 --> 01:07:59.610
which set of documents to keep.

01:07:59.610 --> 01:08:03.210
So the large reasoning
models by themselves

01:08:03.210 --> 01:08:06.310
often don't do a
good job in scaling

01:08:06.310 --> 01:08:08.350
well if you put a lot
of documents in there.

01:08:08.350 --> 01:08:12.450
So this particular approach
allows you to improve on that.

01:08:12.450 --> 01:08:15.330
And larger models
often do better.

01:08:15.330 --> 01:08:19.670
And then-- OK, there's another
paper that I wanted to cover,

01:08:19.670 --> 01:08:21.109
but I didn't end up covering.

01:08:21.109 --> 01:08:24.170
So the Search-R1
stuff effectively

01:08:24.170 --> 01:08:28.010
varies from Search-o1 in that
it teaches the model how to go

01:08:28.010 --> 01:08:29.750
search in an automatic way.

01:08:29.750 --> 01:08:34.979
So what I wanted to say here was
that Search-R1 versus Search-o1.

01:08:34.979 --> 01:08:38.260
Search-o1 is closing the loop
with a prompting-based approach.

01:08:38.260 --> 01:08:40.560
Search-R1 can actually
teach the model

01:08:40.560 --> 01:08:45.840
to do this with a reinforcement
learning-based loop.

01:08:45.840 --> 01:08:48.279
We will not cover
this paper today

01:08:48.279 --> 01:08:49.979
because we are
basically at the end,

01:08:49.979 --> 01:08:52.620
but it's worth going and
taking a look at that paper.

01:08:56.439 --> 01:08:58.560
Questions?

01:08:58.560 --> 01:08:59.960
Yes.

01:08:59.960 --> 01:09:02.800
I was just following this
poster during the definition

01:09:02.800 --> 01:09:03.979
of a language model.

01:09:03.979 --> 01:09:07.920
So when we generate
an answer, are we

01:09:07.920 --> 01:09:09.880
supposed to have
the ability to put

01:09:09.880 --> 01:09:13.080
a probability of that answer?

01:09:13.080 --> 01:09:15.620
And I'm just wondering
that when we have this,

01:09:15.620 --> 01:09:23.439
let's say 1,000 generations,
each generation have a different

01:09:23.439 --> 01:09:26.479
probability, or at
least in some extent.

01:09:26.479 --> 01:09:29.580
And has there been
some correlation

01:09:29.580 --> 01:09:34.840
between the right answers and
the probabilities, or they are--

01:09:34.840 --> 01:09:37.859
by definition, they are
also a bit uncorrelated.

01:09:37.859 --> 01:09:44.160
And also, same question to
RAG, there's got to be--

01:09:44.160 --> 01:09:49.420
I have a RAG
augmented generation.

01:09:49.420 --> 01:09:52.380
I can imagine that
some of the-- maybe it

01:09:52.380 --> 01:09:54.700
messes up some of
the definition.

01:09:54.700 --> 01:10:01.940
Is there any difference in
the probability of the answer?

01:10:01.940 --> 01:10:03.880
I mean, that's a great
experiment to go do.

01:10:03.880 --> 01:10:05.640
So let me answer
the first question.

01:10:05.640 --> 01:10:07.015
And the second
question, you will

01:10:07.015 --> 01:10:09.420
have to go experiment yourself.

01:10:09.420 --> 01:10:12.660
So in the first question,
typically-- so there's

01:10:12.660 --> 01:10:15.700
a lot of studies, but if you
were to just take the output

01:10:15.700 --> 01:10:18.540
generation and compute the log
probabilities of the output

01:10:18.540 --> 01:10:22.440
tokens and aggregate them
in some meaningful way,

01:10:22.440 --> 01:10:23.940
not just sum them,
you'll have to do

01:10:23.940 --> 01:10:26.580
some averaging or some
geometric product or something.

01:10:26.580 --> 01:10:31.130
What you'll find is that the
models tend to be overconfident

01:10:31.130 --> 01:10:33.390
about what they--

01:10:33.390 --> 01:10:35.370
overconfident, so if you
were to calibrate that

01:10:35.370 --> 01:10:37.130
to the right
answer, often you'll

01:10:37.130 --> 01:10:41.810
find that the correctness is--

01:10:41.810 --> 01:10:44.790
Like if it's 50% correct, it
will still be overconfident.

01:10:44.790 --> 01:10:47.190
It'll be like 80% very
confident that this is correct.

01:10:47.190 --> 01:10:50.650
And this shows up
in the ways in which

01:10:50.650 --> 01:10:52.510
if you try to correct
the model outputs,

01:10:52.510 --> 01:10:55.230
it will sometimes be
pretty strong in like, no,

01:10:55.230 --> 01:10:58.110
this is the right answer and
will not change its mind.

01:10:58.110 --> 01:11:02.690
So in some ways, the
model outputs do tend--

01:11:02.690 --> 01:11:06.210
have exhibited not
high calibration

01:11:06.210 --> 01:11:09.570
and are more on the
overconfident side.

01:11:09.570 --> 01:11:11.730
And there have been
research to try

01:11:11.730 --> 01:11:13.970
to get the model to
estimate the confidence, say

01:11:13.970 --> 01:11:17.030
in a second pass or in a--

01:11:17.030 --> 01:11:22.330
and try to do RL or RLHF on
top of that to get the model

01:11:22.330 --> 01:11:27.110
to calibrate and do things
so that it doesn't actually

01:11:27.110 --> 01:11:29.910
emit answers that it
has low confidence over.

01:11:29.910 --> 01:11:33.455
So there has definitely been
efforts in that direction.

01:11:33.455 --> 01:11:35.330
Different models exhibit
different behaviors.

01:11:35.330 --> 01:11:38.870
We don't know
which lab did what.

01:11:38.870 --> 01:11:43.350
So we just get these notions of
oh, the model is hallucinating.

01:11:43.350 --> 01:11:46.910
There is no correlation between
the actual right answer both

01:11:46.910 --> 01:11:49.110
from the 1,000 and the--

01:11:49.110 --> 01:11:51.950
I think there is a
correlation in that

01:11:51.950 --> 01:11:55.470
if the answer is correct,
then the model will likely

01:11:55.470 --> 01:11:56.250
get it correct.

01:11:56.250 --> 01:11:58.230
But if the answer is
incorrect, the model

01:11:58.230 --> 01:11:59.870
might be overconfident
in feeling

01:11:59.870 --> 01:12:01.590
that it knows the answer.

01:12:01.590 --> 01:12:04.450
That's been the
observed pattern,

01:12:04.450 --> 01:12:07.950
but I think this
is worth studying.

01:12:07.950 --> 01:12:10.230
The hallucinations
in just the ability

01:12:10.230 --> 01:12:12.070
of the model to
know what they know

01:12:12.070 --> 01:12:15.990
is an active area of research.

01:12:15.990 --> 01:12:19.190
And it's actually a great
set of research projects

01:12:19.190 --> 01:12:21.900
if someone wants to pursue it.

